<header class="ref-head"><span class="hero-ornament"></span><h1 class="research-title">8 July in history</h1><p class="ref-lede">8 events that happened on 8 July, from 1099 to 1889.</p></header>
<ol class="day-events"><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1099">1099 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">927 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Crusaders process barefoot around the walls of Jerusalem</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Weakened by thirst and heat, the crusaders fasted and walked barefoot around besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, ending on the Mount of Olives. A week later their final assault took the city.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1167">1167 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">859 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Byzantines decisively defeat the Hungarians at Sirmium</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">The Byzantine army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-i-komnenos">Manuel I Komnenos</a>, under Andronikos Kontostephanos, decisively defeated the Hungarians near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sirmium">Sirmium</a>, forcing them to sue for peace on Byzantine terms. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a> was left in control of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syrmia">Syrmia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dalmatia">Dalmatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> for a time.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sirmium">Battle of Sirmium</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1283">1283 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">743 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Roger of Lauria defeats an Angevin fleet at Malta</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">An Aragonese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a> defeated Angevin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> at the entrance to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>'s Grand Harbour during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicilian-vespers">War of the Sicilian Vespers</a>. The Angevin fleet had been sent to put down a rebellion on the island.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roger-of-lauria">Roger of Lauria</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">House of Anjou</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1497">1497 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">529 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Vasco da Gama sails from Lisbon for India</h2><p class="tih-event-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a> left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> with four ships on a voyage that rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a> and reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a> in 1498. The sea route to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> launched Portugal's maritime empire in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag tih-tag-profile" href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-da-gama">Vasco da Gama</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calicut">Calicut</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1709">1709 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">317 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Peter the Great crushes the Swedish army at Poltava</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Russian forces destroyed the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-xii-of-sweden">Charles XII</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-poltava">Poltava</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. The battle ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>'s era as a great power and announced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as the dominant force in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card tih-has-thumb tih-event-hero"><p class="tih-hero-kicker">&#10022; Anniversary of the day</p><a class="tih-event-thumb" href="/encyclopedia/philadelphia" tabindex="-1" aria-hidden="true"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philadelphia.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="168" height="112"></a><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1776">1776 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">250 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Declaration of Independence is first read in public in Philadelphia</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Colonel John Nixon read the Declaration to a crowd in the State House yard, now Independence Square. Tradition holds that the Liberty Bell rang out, though this story dates from the nineteenth century.</p></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1853">1853 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">173 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">Commodore Perry's 'Black Ships' arrive in Edo Bay</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">US <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Commodore Matthew Perry</a> anchored four warships off Uraga and demanded that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> open its ports. The visit ended two centuries of restricted contact and set Japan on the path to rapid modernisation.</p><div class="tih-tags"><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-ships">Black Ships</a><a class="tih-tag enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edo-bay">Edo Bay</a></div></div></li><li class="tih-event-card"><div class="tih-event-main"><p class="tih-event-year"><a href="/years/1889">1889 CE</a> <span class="tih-ago">137 years ago</span></p><h2 class="tih-event-title">The Wall Street Journal publishes its first issue</h2><p class="tih-event-desc">Dow Jones and Company, founded by Charles Dow, Edward Jones and Charles Bergstresser, launched the Wall Street Journal. It became one of the world's most influential financial newspapers.</p></div></li></ol>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-born"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-born">Born on 8 July <span class="n">2</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Emmanuel Philibert, Duke of Savoy</span><span class="tp">1528–1580</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-von-zeppelin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/ferdinand-von-zeppelin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Ferdinand von Zeppelin</span><span class="tp">1838–1917</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<section class="day-ppl" aria-labelledby="day-died"><h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="day-died">Died on 8 July <span class="n">7</span></h2><ul class="enc-seealso"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/pepin-of-italy"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pepin-of-italy.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Pepin of Italy</span><span class="tp">777–810</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/theobald-i-of-navarre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/theobald-i-of-navarre.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Theobald I of Navarre</span><span class="tp">1201–1253</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-almagro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/diego-de-almagro.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Diego de Almagro</span><span class="tp">c. 1475–1538</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/christiaan-huygens"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/christiaan-huygens.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Christiaan Huygens</span><span class="tp">1629–1695</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/joseph-ward"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/joseph-ward.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Joseph Ward</span><span class="tp">1856–1930</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/jean-moulin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jean-moulin.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Jean Moulin</span><span class="tp">1899–1943</span></span></a></li><li><a href="/encyclopedia/kim-il-sung"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/kim-il-sung.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Kim Il Sung</span><span class="tp">1912–1994</span></span></a></li></ul></section>
<p class="day-ppl-note">Dates as the sources give them: in the Julian calendar before 1582, and in some countries for longer (Britain until 1752, Russia until 1918). Only people whose day of birth or death the records agree on are listed.</p>
